From 06968b889f2b81bc81eb16121803fb0ed7c78f8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 13:00:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2014/2064] ApplicationPlay: don't let a zero resume point mask
 the one in the database

GetOptionsAndUpdateItem() used CFileItem::IsResumePointSet() to decide whether
the item already carried a resume position, and only consulted
CVideoDatabase::GetResumeBookMark() when it did not. That test is
CBookmark::IsSet(), which checks totalTimeInSeconds alone, so a resume point
holding a position of zero alongside a known duration counts as set.

Add-ons produce exactly that shape. plugin.video.youtube calls
InfoTagVideo.setResumePoint(0, duration) on the item it returns from
setResolvedUrl whenever its own playback history holds no position for the
video, and CVideoInfoTag::Merge() copies it over the item's real resume point
while CPluginDirectory resolves the plugin path, as that gates on IsSet() too.
The zero then satisfied the check here, so starttime was taken as zero and the
resume bookmark in the database - correct all along - was never read. Playback
restarted from the beginning, and because the two stores are written
independently the video kept restarting from the beginning on every attempt.

Require an actual position instead. GetCurrentResumeTime() is already called on
the following line, so this keeps one notion of a usable resume position, and a
zero now falls through to the database lookup as intended. The existing
starttime == 0.0 guard below still clears the stale resume request when no
source has a position to offer.

(cherry picked from commit e4948a72f82213ff4465edd85ad26b6394d2c81c)
---
 xbmc/application/ApplicationPlay.cpp | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/application/ApplicationPlay.cpp b/xbmc/application/ApplicationPlay.cpp
index 3ffbab621d..818e5037d1 100644
--- a/xbmc/application/ApplicationPlay.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/application/ApplicationPlay.cpp
@@ -141,8 +141,16 @@ void CApplicationPlay::GetOptionsAndUpdateItem()
     {
       m_options.starttime = 0.0;
 
-      // See if resume point is set in the item
-      if (m_item.IsResumePointSet())
+      // See if a usable resume point is set in the item.
+      // Deliberately not CFileItem::IsResumePointSet(): CBookmark::IsSet() only
+      // tests totalTimeInSeconds, so a resume point carrying a position of zero
+      // alongside a known duration counts as "set". Add-ons produce exactly that
+      // when they stamp InfoTagVideo.setResumePoint(0, duration) on the item they
+      // return from setResolvedUrl, and CVideoInfoTag::Merge() then copies it over
+      // the item's real resume point during plugin path resolution. Trusting it
+      // here would mask the resume position held in the database and restart
+      // playback from the beginning, so require an actual position instead.
+      if (m_item.GetCurrentResumeTime() > 0.0)
       {
         m_options.starttime = m_item.GetCurrentResumeTime();
         if (m_item.HasVideoInfoTag())
